## 2. Understand the execution levels

| YAML level | Simple meaning | Example from the reference |
|---|---|---|
| Pipeline | Connects workflows into a larger process. | `release-pipeline` in the separate stage-based example below. |
| Stage | Groups workflows into a phase in a stage-based pipeline. | `test-stage` in that example. |
| Workflow | A named job that runs steps and can call other workflows. | `qa_android` in v1.10.0. |
| Step | One operation inside a workflow. | `android-build@1` produces the APK. |
| Script | Bash commands the runner executes from a Script step. | The Node setup commands under `inputs → content`. |

### What the shared `bitrise.yml` runs

Platform Engineering maintains these operations in the shared template. A **Script step** contains Bash under `inputs → content`. A **Bitrise step** runs a packaged operation such as Git checkout or Xcode archive. The selected workflow determines which operations Bitrise runs.

| Operation in `react-native-qa-uat` v1.10.0 | Where to find it | What runs |
|---|---|---|
| Check out the app | `_checkout` | Bitrise steps activate the SSH key when supplied and clone the app repository when the checkout condition allows it. |
| Set up Node | `_prepare_node` | Two Bash scripts clean up version files in the build checkout and select the configured Node version with `asdf`. |
| Authenticate to the package registry | `_configure_sprout_npm_auth` | Bash configures npm access for private packages using the approved runtime token. |
| Install dependencies | `_npm_install_standard`, `_npm_install_legacy_peer_deps` | npm steps provide two installation modes. The shown `qa_android` workflow selects the legacy peer-dependency mode. |
| Prepare app environment values | `_write_env_file` | Bash writes the configured environment values into the build checkout when applicable. |
| Restore and save caches | `_restore_node_cache`, `_restore_android_cache`, `_restore_ios_cache` and matching `_save_*` workflows | Bitrise cache steps reuse and save eligible dependency or build files. |
| Build the Android APK | `_android_build` | Bitrise steps set up Java and Android tools, set the version, and build the APK. An inline Bash script handles the ephemeral Android debug keystore. |
| Archive the iOS app | `_ios_archive` | Bitrise steps install CocoaPods dependencies, set the build number, and archive with the configured export method and signing. |
| Run Android and iOS builds together | `qa_parallel`, `uat_parallel` | The build router launches the corresponding Android and iOS child workflows and waits for their results. |
| Publish a private Bitrise artifact | `_bitrise_upload`, also used by `verify_bitrise_artifact_upload` | The upload step publishes build evidence in Bitrise with the public artifact page disabled. |
| Deliver to Digital.ai App Management | `_apperian_deploy` | Bash handles update, upload, publication, policy processing, destination signing, and enablement as configured. `SKIP_DEPLOY` defaults to `true`. |

### Checks and destinations that depend on the selected template

The supplied v1.10.0 YAML shows the operations above. Use the approved template version to confirm which of the following integrations your app has and where they run.

| Integration | What to look for in the selected workflow |
|---|---|
| Danger PR checks | The PR workflow invokes the shared Danger rules and posts feedback to GitHub. |
| Lint, type checks, unit tests, and coverage | Steps call the approved checks for the app and report their results. |
| Parallel test execution | Test workflows run selected suites or Jest shards and report each required result. |
| Sauce Labs | Steps prepare and upload the required app and test packages, run device tests, and collect the completed result. |
| NowSecure | Steps submit the supported binary, wait for its assessment, and evaluate the required severity policy. |
| Google Play or App Store Connect | An approved delivery step sends the signed candidate to the configured destination and route. |

### Follow one real workflow from the supplied template

The React Native v1.10.0 sample defines its jobs under `workflows:`. Start with `qa_android` and follow the names in `before_run` and `after_run` to their definitions elsewhere in the same file. This excerpt retains the source indentation. It is one entry under `workflows:` and depends on the other definitions in the template.

```yaml
  qa_android:
    description: Android QA build and artifact upload.
    before_run:
    - _checkout
    - _restore_node_cache
    - _prepare_node
    - _configure_sprout_npm_auth
    - _npm_install_legacy_peer_deps
    - _write_env_file
    after_run:
    - _restore_android_cache
    - _android_build
    - _save_node_cache
    - _save_android_cache
    - _bitrise_upload
    - _apperian_deploy
    envs:
    - DEPLOY_ENV: qa
      opts:
        is_expand: false
    - DEPLOY_PLATFORMS: android
      opts:
        is_expand: false
    - SKIP_POLICIES: true
      opts:
        is_expand: false
```

| Read this part | What happens in the QA Android run |
|---|---|
| `before_run` | Check out the app, restore the Node cache, prepare Node and package access, install dependencies, and prepare the app environment. |
| `after_run` | Restore the Android cache, build the APK, save caches, upload the Bitrise artifact, and reach the Digital.ai deployment workflow. |
| `envs` | Set the environment to QA and the platform to Android for this route. |

The iOS route uses `_ios_archive` in place of `_android_build`. Its archive depends on the selected workspace, scheme, export settings, and signing bindings. The UAT workflows select the UAT environment.

### Read an inline script

This exact Script step from `_prepare_node` shows how YAML hands Bash commands to the runner.

```yaml
    - script@1:
        title: Set Node version
        run_if: '{{getenv "SKIP_TOOL_DOWNLOAD_STEPS" | ne "true"}}'
        inputs:
        - content: |-
            #!/usr/bin/env bash
            set -euo pipefail
            set -x

            asdf install nodejs "$NODE_VERSION"
            asdf global nodejs "$NODE_VERSION"
```

`run_if` decides whether the step runs. `content: |-` supplies the indented Bash commands. Here, `asdf` installs and selects the Node version in `NODE_VERSION`. The runner must already have the expected tool available.

### Follow the parallel Android and iOS builds

The `qa_parallel` workflow prepares shared dependencies and uses the following router step to start two child builds. The UAT workflow follows the same pattern for its UAT children.

```yaml
    - build-router-start@0.14.7:
        title: Start QA platform builds
        inputs:
        - access_token: "$BITRISE_START_BUILD_ACCESS_TOKEN"
        - workflows: |-
            qa_android_parallel
            qa_ios_parallel
        - environment_key_list: |-
            WORKDIR
            PROJECT_LOCATION
            MODULE
            VARIANT
            IOS_PROJECT_PATH
            IOS_SCHEME
            IOS_DISTRIBUTION_METHOD
            IOS_PODFILE_PATH
            IOS_PLIST_PATH
            NODE_VERSION
            ENV_FILE_KEYS
            SKIP_DEPLOY
            SKIP_SIGNING
            SKIP_ENABLE
            REMOVE_POLICIES_AFTER_DEPLOY
            SKIP_TOOL_DOWNLOAD_STEPS
        - wait_for_builds: "true"
        - abort_on_fail: "yes"
        - verbose: "no"
```

Read each child build's result separately. The parent passes the listed environment keys and waits for the child builds. The child workflows check out the app and restore their caches. This is **parallel platform builds**. For changed-module selection or splitting a Jest suite, see [Parallel Testing](/mobile/cicd/parallel-testing/).

In v1.10.0, the private artifact upload and Digital.ai delivery have separate outcomes. `SKIP_DEPLOY` defaults to `true`, so a build can publish its Bitrise artifact and skip Digital.ai. Confirm the actual destination and exact version in [Deploy & Verify](/mobile/cicd/deploy-and-verify/).

