## 5. Read the separate Android and Sauce Labs integration example

Platform Engineering maintains this workflow and its Sauce Labs automation. The request path shown in the reference is a runtime input. Confirm how the selected template makes the request and runner configuration available on the Bitrise runner.

The following `deploy` workflow is reproduced exactly from the integration reference. The supplied v1.10.0 sample omits Danger, Jest sharding, Sauce Labs, and NowSecure. Platform Engineering supplies those integrations when the selected template version includes them. Read the prerequisites below before adapting this example.

```yaml
workflows:
  deploy:
    steps:
      # 1. Clone the repository
      - git-clone@8: {}

      # 2. Install dependencies
      - npm@3:
          title: npm install
          inputs:
            - workdir: "$WORKDIR"
            - command: install

      # 3. Run unit tests
      - npm@3:
          title: npm test
          inputs:
            - workdir: "$WORKDIR"
            - command: test

      # 4. Set up Android build tools
      - install-missing-android-tools@3: {}

      # 5. Build the Android app
      - android-build@1:
          inputs:
            - project_location: "$PROJECT_LOCATION"
            - module: "$MODULE"
            - variant: "$VARIANT"

      # 6. [Optional] Install saucectl for running tests
      - script@1:
          inputs:
            - content: |-
                npm install -g saucectl
                saucectl --version

      # 7. Register / provision the app in Sauce Labs
      - git::https://git.enterprise.com/ACES-Mobile-Engineering/bitrise-step-saucelabs-app-create.git@1.0.0:
          title: Create Sauce Labs App
          inputs:
            - saucelabs_request_yml: ".sauce/saucelabs_request.yml"
            - sauce_username: "$SAUCE_USERNAME"
            - sauce_access_key: "$SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY"

      # 8. Upload the built APK and run tests
      - git::https://git.enterprise.com/ACES-Mobile-Engineering/saucelabs-bitrise-step.git@1.0.0:
          title: Upload App to Sauce Labs and Run Tests
          inputs:
            - sauce_username: "$SAUCE_USERNAME"
            - sauce_access_key: "$SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY"
```

### Follow each step

| Step | Where it runs | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `git-clone@8` | Bitrise runner. | Checks out application source using the configured repository access. |
| `npm@3` with `command: install` | Bitrise runner, in `$WORKDIR`. | Installs the application's dependencies using the supplied npm command. |
| `npm@3` with `command: test` | Bitrise runner, in `$WORKDIR`. | Runs the test script defined in `package.json`. |
| `install-missing-android-tools@3` | Android-capable runner. | Prepares Android tools required by this example. |
| `android-build@1` | Android-capable runner. | Builds the configured project/module/variant. |
| `script@1` | Bitrise runner's shell. | Executes the embedded `saucectl` installation and version commands. |
| Sauce Labs app-create step | Bitrise runner, calling the configured integration. | Registers/provisions the application using request metadata. |
| Sauce Labs upload/test step | Bitrise runner and Sauce Labs service. | Performs the configured package upload and cloud-test operation. |

The source installs an unpinned global `saucectl`. Keep the reference snippet intact for comparison and have the owner confirm the approved CLI version and installation procedure for an executable template.

### Configure the required inputs

| Input | What must be supplied |
|---|---|
| `WORKDIR` | The checked-out directory containing `package.json`. |
| `PROJECT_LOCATION` | Android Gradle project location expected by the pinned build step. |
| `MODULE` | Actual application module for the selected route. |
| `VARIANT` | Actual application build variant. |
| `.sauce/saucelabs_request.yml` | Request file consumed by this example's pinned app-create step. |
| `SAUCE_USERNAME`, `SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY` | Protected Sauce Labs credential bindings. |
